  {
   "turn": 9,
   "response_text": "The maximum validation accuracy achieved was 0.829427 (at epoch 12). The healthy range is 0.8538-0.8599. This means:\n- Maximum achieved: 0.829427\n- Healthy lower bound: 0.8538\n- Gap: -0.024+ (the max is still about 2.4% below healthy range)\n\nThis is a clear anomaly. The model's performance never reaches the healthy range even at its best epoch.\n\nNow I need to determine what could cause this. The key parameter that stands out is:\n\n**label_noise_fraction: 0.42**\n\nThis is an extremely high amount of label noise (42% of labels are flipped). In the context of healthy runs achieving ~0.8568 accuracy without this noise, a 0.42 noise fraction would dramatically reduce model performance - exactly as we're seeing here.\n\nLet me check if this parameter is the culprit by examining the config again and thinking about what a reasonable value would be:\n\nThe configuration shows:\n```\ndata:\n  label_noise_fraction: 0.42\n```\n\nFor the Adult dataset with optimal training, healthy accuracy is 0.8568. With 42% label noise, the validation accuracy would indeed be around 0.82-0.83, which matches our observations exactly.\n\nHowever, comparing to healthy runs that achieve 0.8568, the expected label_noise_fraction for a healthy run should be much lower or 0. The 0.42 value appears to be the fault - it's artificially degrading model performance.\n\nLet me verify this is actually the issue by checking what values would be reasonable. A label noise fraction of 0.42 is extremely high and would definitely explain the ~2.5-3% accuracy drop we're seeing.\n\n**Diagnosis**: The model is configured with an extremely high label_noise_fraction (0.42), which artificially corrupts 42% of the training labels. This is causing the validation accuracy to drop from the expected ~0.8568 to the observed ~0.823-0.829, consistently placing it outside the healthy range. This is the root cause of the anomaly.\n\nThe correct value should likely be 0.0 (no label noise) to match healthy runs, or at most a much smaller fraction if noise is intentionally being tested.",
